import strings.String

//# The `Display` interface: a value as a person reads it, what `"${value}"`
//# and `io.print` write. `Debug` is the developer-facing form; the table of
//# how every built-in type renders in each is on
//# [std::debug](/reference/debug/#how-built-in-values-render).

/// Protocol for converting a value to a `String` a person reads.
///
/// `io.print` and string interpolation (`"${value}"`) write a value's
/// `Display` form, so a type with an impl prints as the built-in types
/// do. The interface a typed literal's handler names for its slot values
/// extends `Display`, so every interpolated value has a text form.
///
/// `Int`, `Float`, `Decimal`, `Bool` and `String` implement `Display`, and
/// so does a collection whose elements do. To implement it for a custom
/// type:
///
/// ```nomi
/// pub struct Greeter
///     name: String
/// end
///
/// impl Display for Greeter
///     fn to_string(g: Greeter) -> String
///         "hi ${g.name}"
///     end
/// end
/// ```
pub interface Display
    fn to_string(value: self) -> String
end
